# Backend Utilities & DRY

Prefer existing `utils/` and shared middleware over copy-paste. If the same pattern appears twice, extract it.

## Required building blocks

| Need | Use | Path |
|------|-----|------|
| Thrown / next() API error | `createError` / `AppError` | `utils/error.js` |
| Error codes | `ErrorCodes.*` | `utils/error.js` |
| 404 handler | `notFound` | `utils/error.js` |
| Global error response | `errorMiddleware` | `utils/error.js` |
| Send error JSON | `sendError` | `utils/error.js` |

Frontend-facing error shape (do not invent a second format):

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "error": { "code": "DEMO_ERROR", "message": "…", "statusCode": 400 }
}
```

Success responses stay consistent: `{ success: true, data?: …, message?: … }`.

## Examples

```js
// ❌ BAD
res.status(400).json({ msg: 'bad' });
throw new Error('cycle sync failed');

// ✅ GOOD
next(createError('cycle sync failed. Please try again.', 400, ErrorCodes.VALIDATION));
// or: throw createError('…', 404, ErrorCodes.NOT_FOUND);
```

```js
// ❌ BAD — duplicate date/cycle math in two controllers
// ✅ GOOD — one function in services/ or utils/, both call it
```

## Rules

1. User-facing messages live on `AppError.message` — safe to show in Flutter snackbars
2. Never leak SQL / stack traces in JSON; log server-side only
3. Controllers: parse → call service → `res.json`; no duplicated try/catch boilerplate if `errorMiddleware` handles it
4. New shared helper → `utils/<name>.js`; export named functions; no god-files
5. Do not add a second error class or response envelope alongside `utils/error.js`
